Most AI assistants answer when you ask. Meta's Muse is pitched as something different, a personal agent that keeps working after you close the app, comes back when something changes, and asks before it does anything sensitive. This post reads three Meta newsroom posts, the September launch, the November voice update and the Australian launch, and sorts out what Muse does, where it is available and which controls Meta says you have.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited pages for figures, dates and wording. This is not a full human review. Meta also builds AI models and assistants that compete with Claude, so read the comparisons with that in mind. Stackbrook did not test Muse or any product mentioned and has no affiliate relationship with any company mentioned at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated.*

## What Muse is

Meta's launch post, dated 8 September 2026, presents Muse as an agent that takes initiative, works toward goals you set and offers ideas of its own, with security and privacy as selling points. It is powered by Muse Spark, which Meta calls its most capable model to date. You talk to it like messaging a person, either in the Muse app or directly in WhatsApp.

The technical idea behind it is a dedicated cloud computer. Meta calls it the Muse Secure VM, a virtual machine that holds the agent and the person's data.

## What it can do

According to the launch post, Muse can handle emails, travel bookings, web browsing and forms, and negotiate on your behalf. It can turn a goal into a personalised plan, keep working on it after you leave, and come back when something changes or when it needs your approval. It can also make suggestions without being asked and remember details. Meta's examples are turning a saved Instagram recipe reel into a grocery list and remembering friends' dietary restrictions before invites go out.

Purchases go through Link, which Meta says is built by Stripe. Muse uses a one-time-use card so your real card details stay hidden, and Meta says Muse is the first AI agent covered by Link's purchase protections. Shop Pay is described as coming soon, and 1Password support is mentioned alongside it with no date given. Those are plans, not features you can use today, so treat them as unconfirmed until Meta says they have shipped.

## Voice and avatars

Meta's 5 November post says real-time voice conversations, which it previewed at Connect 2026, were rolling out in the Muse app in the US to start. A voice chat starts from a phone icon at the top right of the app. The avatar reacts while it listens and speaks with expressions and gestures, and you can show voice only, the avatar next to the conversation, or full screen.

Meta says there are hundreds of voices to choose from and you can change them at any time. You can interrupt Muse while it is speaking, and Meta says it can handle tasks in the background while you talk, for example while you are out on a walk. The post lists brainstorming, trip planning and rehearsing an interview or a presentation as example uses. The same post says the Muse app with voice runs on mobile, web and Mac, and that voice conversations are coming soon to AI Glasses and Muse Charm, with no dates given. It does not describe Muse Charm.

## Where it is available

![Timeline of three Meta Muse announcements: launch on 8 September, voice and avatars on 5 November, Australia on 17 November](/images/site1/meta-muse-personal-agent-voice-explained-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the cited pages.*

The launch post says Muse is rolling out in the US, on iOS, Android and muse.ai, with AI glasses listed as coming soon. Meta's 17 November post says Muse is available in Australia from that day, on iOS, Android, muse.ai and a Mac app. In Australia, Muse can connect to apps such as Gmail, Google Calendar, Instagram and OpenTable to manage email, coordinate schedules and make restaurant reservations. Meta names early connector partners including Canva (which it says is also a connector worldwide), Domain, Kogan, MECCA, Officeworks, realestate.com.au, SWEAT, ShopBack and Ticketek, and says those connector features will roll out progressively, with some experiences arriving after launch. The post also lists real-time voice conversations on mobile, web or Mac in Australia, so voice is not limited to the US as of that post, and Spaces, a custom experience you create inside Muse by describing it. AI glasses are again listed as coming soon.

The three posts name only the US and Australia as places Muse is available. Their related-news lists also link to a Singapore launch, which we did not read, so other countries may exist. The voice post does not state languages.

## What it costs

The launch post says Muse is free for most everyday needs, with subscription plans for people who want to do more. The Australia post similarly says most core functions are free and paid plans add capabilities. Neither gives prices, plan names or usage limits. If you plan to rely on Muse for a business task, the honest position today is that the cost of the heavier use is unpublished on the pages we read.

## What you control

Meta's launch post lists several controls, and these are Meta's statements, not tested results. Muse runs on its own cloud computer, and a separate Sentinel agent on the same machine must approve anything Muse sends to the internet. Meta says Muse cannot see passwords or payment methods, because credentials go into secure storage. It says Muse checks with you before sensitive actions such as sending an email or making a purchase, and keeps an audit trail.

You choose which apps Muse connects to and how much access it gets, and you can change access or disconnect a service at any time. Meta says you can opt out of your interactions being used to train its AI models, that Muse does not share conversations or VM data with Meta's ad systems, and that you can tell Muse to forget specific things it has learned. For later in the year, Meta announced a Muse Confidential VM, encrypted with a key only the user holds, so that Meta says it could not access the contents. That is a future plan, not a shipped feature as of the pages we read.

## What the pages do not say

The 5 November voice post contains no safety, privacy or data-retention statements of its own, so how voice recordings are handled is not covered there. None of the three posts gives prices, usage caps, supported languages for voice or independent measurements of how reliable Muse is when it books, buys or negotiates. We did not read the linked research and security pages, so details such as how the Sentinel agent decides what to approve are outside this post.

## A cautious way to approach it

This is general information, not legal or financial advice. If you try an agent that can spend money or send email, start with narrow connections, keep purchase approval switched on, and review the audit trail early on. Because Stackbrook has not tested Muse, every capability above is Meta's description of its own product.
